+(resuming-a-shuffled-epoch)=
+### Resuming a Shuffled Epoch
+
+A job that is interrupted in the middle of an epoch and restarted would
+normally get a new order from {class}`~torch.utils.data.RandomSampler` (the
+sampler behind `shuffle=True`), and so see some samples twice and miss
+others. To avoid this, save the sampler's position with the rest of the
+checkpoint and restore it into the new sampler before iterating:
+
+```python
+loader = DataLoader(dataset, batch_size=32, shuffle=True)
+
+# when checkpointing
+checkpoint["sampler"] = loader.sampler.state_dict()
+
+# after restarting, with a loader built the same way
+loader.sampler.load_state_dict(checkpoint["sampler"])
+for batch in loader:  # the rest of the interrupted epoch, in the same order
+    ...
+```
+
+The next iteration yields exactly the samples the interrupted one had not yet
+yielded, in the same order; the epochs after it are shuffled as usual. The
+state is a picklable `dict` and can be saved with {func}`torch.save`. It must be
+loaded into a sampler over data of the same length, with the same
+`replacement` and `num_samples`. If the sampler has a `generator`, its state is
+saved and restored too, so the later epochs are the ones the uninterrupted run
+would have drawn.
+
+:::{note}
+The saved position counts the indices the *sampler* has handed out. With
+`num_workers > 0`, a {class}`~torch.utils.data.DataLoader` fetches up to
+`prefetch_factor * num_workers` batches ahead of the ones it has returned, so
+that count is ahead of what the training loop has received, and resuming from
+it would skip the prefetched samples. To resume from what the loop consumed,
+set `state["yielded"]` to the number of samples of the current epoch the loop
+has received before loading the state (this relies on the default
+`in_order=True`, under which batches are returned in sampler order):
+
+```python
+state = loader.sampler.state_dict()
+state["yielded"] = min(state["yielded"], batches_this_epoch * batch_size)
+```
+:::
+
